// A transition argument of COMPOSITE type routes only when it is a LOCAL.
// Scalars route from anywhere.
//
//   let v: Pair = Pair { a: 1 };  handle(v)        checks
//   handle(Pair { a: 1 })         (a literal)      OMITTED (this fixture)
//   handle(self.pair)             (a field read)   OMITTED
//   handle(self.give())           (a value call)   OMITTED
//   handle(self.n)   / handle(7)  (scalars)        check
//
// all omissions at `state graph: terminator: jump successor: parameter
// transfer`, in both the unconditional `_ ->` and the guarded `true ->` forms.
//
// CORRECTION. An earlier version of this fixture, and the board item filed
// with it, said "a state parameter whose type is not a scalar has no route".
// That is WRONG and was landed. It was measured only with a composite LITERAL
// argument, and a local of the identical type passes. The parameter's type is
// half the condition; the argument's PROVENANCE is the other half, and the
// original reading would have sent a repair at the parameter list, where
// nothing is broken.
//
// What is still true from that measurement: an arithmetic policy on a SCALAR
// parameter (`i32 in Wrapping`) routes, and `[copy]` on the composite changes
// nothing -- so this is neither about policies nor about copyability.
//
// A reference to a composite (`&Pair`) and a fixed array (`[i32; 3]`) omit on
// the same literal-argument reading; whether they pass from a local was not
// re-measured after the correction, so do not assume the local exemption
// covers them.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Pair [copy] {
    a: i32;
}

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    transition { _ -> handle(Pair { a: 1 }) }

    state handle(&mut self, pair: Pair) {
        self.console.exit_process(0);
    }
}
